WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.805
In the 1300s, the poet Dante
Alighieri imagined hell as

00:00:03.855 --> 00:00:07.660
a series of circles going
down deep into the earth.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.650
In the seventh circle, as one
of the worst sins, on burning

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.120
sand with fire falling from the
sky, he imagined the moneylenders.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.620
Each one had a pouch hanging
from his neck, marked

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.660
with his family's coat of
arms, and they stare at

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.700
those pouches forever while
fire rains down from the sky

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.760
and burns them.

00:00:28.577 --> 00:00:31.367
Their sin was lending money at interest.

00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:34.857
If you have a savings account,
your bank pays you interest,

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:37.927
which makes you, in a small
way, a moneylender too.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:41.537
Would Dante have thought
that you should be

00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:44.687
there, in hell, with fire
raining down everywhere?

00:00:45.157 --> 00:00:48.967
Why did so many people, for a
long time, think he was right?

00:00:50.043 --> 00:00:52.253
Interest is rent that gets paid on money.

00:00:52.303 --> 00:00:54.093
So let's look at why renting out

00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:55.933
money, of all things, seemed so wrong.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.440
Start with the Greek philosopher
Aristotle, in the 300s BC.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.000
To him, money was a tool for trading,
and a tool can't have children.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.830
He wrote that money
was intended to be used

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.320
in exchange, but not to
increase at interest.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.840
Interest, he said, is the
birth of money from money, and

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.640
of all the ways to get
rich, the most unnatural.

00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:20.053
The Jewish Torah draws a different line.

00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:21.713
The book of Deuteronomy says,

00:01:22.363 --> 00:01:25.373
You shall not lend on
interest to your brother,

00:01:25.423 --> 00:01:28.433
and then you may charge
a foreigner interest.

00:01:29.443 --> 00:01:32.603
Leviticus adds, If your brother
has become poor, take no

00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:35.813
interest from him, that your
brother may live among you.

00:01:36.303 --> 00:01:39.623
A loan to a neighbor in trouble
was meant to keep him on

00:01:39.673 --> 00:01:42.993
his feet, not to earn a profit from
him, which was considered exploitative.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.263
The Catholic Church went further.

00:01:45.713 --> 00:01:48.672
In 325, the Council of Nicaea said that

00:01:48.722 --> 00:01:51.683
priests who lent at
interest would be removed.

00:01:52.073 --> 00:01:55.223
In 1179, a council in Rome refused

00:01:55.273 --> 00:01:58.422
known moneylenders communion
and a Christian burial.

00:01:58.812 --> 00:02:01.916
And in 1311, the Council
of Vienne said that anyone

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:05.069
who stubbornly insisted on
interest and said it was not

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:08.222
a sin should be treated as a heretic.

00:02:08.273 --> 00:02:11.352
To the scholars of the time, usury meant

00:02:11.403 --> 00:02:14.482
any interest on a loan, high or low.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.090
So how did anyone get a loan?

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.510
Look at Florence, home to several of

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.030
Dante's moneylenders and
to Europe's great bankers.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.480
They used a bill of exchange,
a written order, to pay money

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.470
in another city, in that city's
currency, at a later date.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.170
A London banker might pay
44 pennies for each Venetian

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.870
ducat to be paid in
Venice three months later.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.900
Three months after that,
the money comes back

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.450
to London at 47 pennies per ducat.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.830
That's three pennies gain on
44, about 7% in six months.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.040
Church scholars mostly accepted
it as a trade because exchange

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.730
rates could move, so the
profit was not certain.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.170
The Medici bank kept an account
for profit and loss on exchange.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.470
It had no account for interest.

00:03:05.397 --> 00:03:07.767
Slowly but surely, the line moved.

00:03:08.277 --> 00:03:12.127
The reformer, John Calvin, allowed
interest on business loans, but said

00:03:12.177 --> 00:03:16.027
it should never be demanded
of poor and needy men.

00:03:16.497 --> 00:03:21.487
In 1545, England's Parliament
allowed interest up to 10% a year.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.700
Islam kept the ban.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.710
The Quran even answers the people who

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.720
said interest was no different from trade.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.480
Quote, They say trade is
just like interest, but

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.600
Allah has permitted trade
and has forbidden interest.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:38.080
So Islamic finance is built
on trade and partnership.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.460
To buy a house, a financier can buy it and

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.420
sell it to you at a
higher price paid monthly.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:47.230
Or it can buy the house with you and

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.440
rent you its share until
you've bought it out.

00:03:50.043 --> 00:03:51.773
And the argument still isn't over.

00:03:51.823 --> 00:03:54.653
In 2025, a bill in the U.S.

00:03:54.803 --> 00:03:57.293
Senate proposed capping
credit card interest at 10%.

00:03:57.343 --> 00:04:00.883
And in 2026, the President
called for a one-year cap at

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:04.473
the same rate, the same number
England allowed, in 1545.

00:04:05.223 --> 00:04:06.953
So is charging interest evil?

00:04:07.483 --> 00:04:09.473
These traditions never fully agreed.

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.353
Aristotle and the medieval Church
objected to any interest at all.

00:04:13.583 --> 00:04:14.693
And Islam still does.

00:04:14.743 --> 00:04:16.973
The Torah drew its sharpest line around

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:19.253
a neighbor who had become poor.

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:22.713
And Calvin drew his
around the poor and needy.

00:04:23.143 --> 00:04:25.683
Much of the argument, then and now, is

00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:28.273
about who is doing the borrowing and why.
